<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<PackageManifest Version="2.0.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/vsx-schema/2011" xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/vsx-schema-design/2011">
  <Metadata>
    <Identity Id="SsmsSqlFormatter.7f3d2a1e-5b8c-4e9f-a6d0-1c4b7e2f9a35" Version="2.2.2" Language="en-US" Publisher="Shane" />
    <DisplayName>SSMS SQL Formatter (Rules + AI)</DisplayName>
    <Description xml:space="preserve">Format T-SQL and export results to Excel, without leaving SSMS.

FORMATTING (Ctrl+Shift+Alt+F, or right-click &gt; Format T-SQL Script)
Built on Microsoft's ScriptDom parser, so it understands standard T-SQL and never modifies a script it can't fully parse. Classic (compact) and Modern (expanded) presets, or set every rule individually: keyword, function and data type casing, indentation with spaces or tabs, leading or trailing commas, per-clause line breaks, subquery re-indentation, and exact blank-line control between statements and around GO. Comments are preserved and re-attached to the code they belong to - never silently deleted. An optional AI engine (your own Anthropic API key) follows free-form style instructions.

EXPORT RESULTS TO EXCEL (Ctrl+Shift+Alt+X)
Click in the results grid, press Ctrl+A, then Ctrl+Shift+Alt+X - a styled .xlsx workbook opens, with a frozen header row and AutoFilter dropdowns. Leading zeros and long IDs stay intact instead of becoming scientific notation or dates. Choose fonts, colours, borders and banded rows; export to CSV instead; write to a folder of your choice; optionally include the query on its own sheet. Ctrl+Shift+Alt+A queues extra result sets so a multi-result query becomes one workbook with a sheet per set.

Settings live under Tools &gt; Options &gt; Format T-SQL Script, and can be exported and imported as JSON to share a house style. Free and open source (MIT).</Description>
    <Tags>SQL;T-SQL;Formatter;SSMS;AI</Tags>
  </Metadata>
  <Installation>
    <!-- SSMS 21 and SSMS 22 (64-bit, VS 2022 based) -->
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Ssms" Version="[21.0,23.0)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
    <!-- Visual Studio 2022, useful for F5 debugging in the experimental instance -->
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Community" Version="[17.0,18.0)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Pro" Version="[17.0,18.0)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Enterprise" Version="[17.0,18.0)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
  </Installation>
  <Dependencies>
    <Dependency Id="Microsoft.Framework.NDP" DisplayName="Microsoft .NET Framework" d:Source="Manual" Version="[4.8,)" />
  </Dependencies>
  <Prerequisites>
    <Prerequisite Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.CoreEditor" Version="[17.0,)" DisplayName="Visual Studio core editor" />
  </Prerequisites>
  <Assets>
    <Asset Type="Microsoft.VisualStudio.VsPackage" d:Source="Project" d:ProjectName="%CurrentProject%" Path="|%CurrentProject%;PkgdefProjectOutputGroup|" />
  </Assets>
</PackageManifest>
